


IAM Policy Document: Three-Tier Web Application (Google Cloud Platform)

1. Purpose

This document defines the Identity and Access Management (IAM) policy for a hypothetical three-tier web application deployed on Google Cloud Platform (GCP). The design follows the principle of least privilege by granting each identity only the permissions required to perform its responsibilities.

2. Application Architecture

Users
  |
External Load Balancer
  |
Frontend (Presentation Tier)
  |
Backend API (Application Tier)
  |
Database (Data Tier)

Supporting Services:
• Cloud Storage
• Cloud Logging
• Cloud Monitoring
• IAM

3. IAM Principals and Roles

3.1 Cloud Administrator
Role(s): roles/owner
Responsibilities: Project configuration, billing, IAM administration, and emergency operations.
Why no broader role was appropriate: The Owner role is the highest project-level role and is limited to a small number of trusted administrators.


3.2 Frontend Service Account
Role(s): roles/storage.objectViewer
Responsibilities: Reads static assets such as images, CSS, and JavaScript files from Cloud Storage.
Why no broader role was appropriate: It only requires read access. Storage Admin would allow deleting buckets and changing permissions.


3.3 Backend API Service Account
Role(s): roles/cloudsql.client
roles/storage.objectCreator
Responsibilities: Connects to Cloud SQL and uploads user-generated content to Cloud Storage.
Why no broader role was appropriate: It does not need database administration or storage administration permissions.


3.4 Database Service Account
Role(s): roles/cloudsql.client
Responsibilities: Provides controlled connectivity to Cloud SQL.
Why no broader role was appropriate: Database administration remains separated from application workloads.


3.5 Security Monitoring Account
Role(s): roles/logging.viewer
Responsibilities: Views logs for monitoring and incident investigation.
Why no broader role was appropriate: Logging Admin permissions are unnecessary because this identity does not manage logging settings.


3.6 Developer Account
Role(s): roles/viewer
Responsibilities: Views project resources, deployments, and logs.
Why no broader role was appropriate: Developers should not modify production resources.


3.7 CI/CD Pipeline Service Account
Role(s): roles/container.developer


roles/storage.objectViewer
Responsibilities: Deploys applications and retrieves deployment artifacts.
Why no broader role was appropriate: Cluster-wide administrative permissions are unnecessary for deployment tasks.


4. Security Principles Applied
•	Least Privilege: Every identity receives only the minimum permissions required.

•	Separation of Duties: Administrative, development, deployment, and monitoring responsibilities are separated.

•	No Shared Accounts: Every user and workload has a unique identity.

•	Reduced Blast Radius: If one identity is compromised, its permissions are limited.


5. Conclusion
This IAM policy demonstrates a secure, least-privilege design for a three-tier web application on GCP. Each principal has a clearly defined purpose, and no identity is granted permissions beyond what is required.
